   "claims": [
    "For example, let’s calculate your hourly pay after a 3% raise if you currently make $20 per hour.",
    "A 3% raise is a typical increase that employers use to help employees keep up with inflation and the increased cost of living.",
    "Enter the $20 hourly rate and 3% raise into the formula above to calculate your new pay rate: new pay = $20 + ($20 × 3%) new pay = $20 + $0.60 new pay = $20.60 So, after a 3% pay raise, you would make $20.60 per hour.",
    "If instead, you were making $30 per hour, a 3% raise would increase your pay rate to $30.90 per hour.",
    "Finally, calculate the annual pay by multiplying the weekly pay by 52 (the number of weeks in a year).",
    "You can also calculate the annual salary by multiplying the pay rates by 2,080, which is the total hours worked in a year, assuming a 40-hour workweek.",
    "Using the example above, let’s calculate your new salary if your pay rate increased from $20 to $20.60 per hour after a 3% pay raise.",
    "Given a 40-hour work week, the calculation for your current pay is: old weekly pay = 40 × $20 = $800 new weekly pay = 40 × $20.60 = $824 Thus, you’d make an extra $24 per week if you got a 3% raise.",
    "For example, let’s calculate the new salary after a 5% raise for an employee with a salary of $40,000.",
    "$40,000 × 0.05 = $2,000 So, this employee’s raise is $2,000 per year."
   ],
